@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About · Weather</PageTitle>

<section class="panel panel--plain">
    <h1 class="panel__title">About this dashboard</h1>
    <p class="panel__body">
        A small weather dashboard built on the U.S. National Weather Service API. It asks your
        browser for your location, resolves it to an NWS forecast grid cell ("tile"), and shows
        everything that cell knows: the daily forecast, the hourly forecast, and the latest
        observation from the nearest station. If you'd rather not share your location, you can
        type coordinates instead — the app works the same either way.
    </p>
    <p class="panel__body">
        Your coordinates are used for two things: to find the tile you're standing in, and to find
        the tiles around you. Because you're rarely at the exact centre of a tile, the dashboard
        also fetches every neighbouring tile and orders them by how close each one is to you — so
        if you're near an edge, the most relevant nearby data is right at the top. Each neighbouring
        tile carries the same full data (daily, hourly, and current conditions). Active watches,
        warnings and advisories for your point are shown at the top when present.
    </p>
    <p class="panel__body">
        Forecasts don't change second to second, so responses are cached locally for a short
        window and reused. That keeps the dashboard quick and keeps the weather service from
        being polled more than it needs to be. The caching is invisible in normal use: you always
        see current data, just faster on repeat views. When the service is briefly unreachable,
        the last good forecast is shown rather than an error.
    </p>
    <p class="panel__body">
        Behind the scenes the app records cache hit/miss ratios and the latency of every call to
        the weather service, so slow responses or rate-limiting show up on a dashboard rather than
        as a mystery. Coordinates are sent only to fetch weather data and aren't stored.
    </p>
    <p class="panel__body">
        The full source is on
        <a href="https://github.com/collabskus/weather" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
</section>
